scala_mcp_fun — wrap a registered Scala.Fun as an mcp tool (tier 1)
====================================================================

status: done (2026-08-19)

spec: "scala-backed mcp tools: scala_mcp_fun and scala_mcp_tool"
(D-2026-08-19-scala-backed-mcp-tools). decided 2026-08-19, NOT scheduled
into a wave. investigation notes: LINTER_FINDINGS.md.

TIER 1 of two. this plan wraps a Scala function that is ALREADY
registered — an entry in some component's Scala.Functions service — as an
ordinary mcp tool. plans/scala_mcp_tool is tier 2 and removes the
"already registered" precondition by reflecting over the classpath; it
depends on this plan and does not restate its steps.

    scala_mcp_fun lint = \<open>my_lint\<close>
        (description \<open>lint a theory\<close>)
        (params theory: string)

ORDERING, and it depends on the goal — read this before assuming tier 1
comes first.

this plan is strictly smaller and needs no new scala code at all. it is
independently useful: it is the whole story for a user who writes their
own scala tool, because they own the jar and can add a Fun.

BUT it does not reach the linter, and it is not on the path to it. the
linter ships no Scala.Functions service, so tier 1 cannot wrap it at any
price; and after D1's rewrite tier 2 no longer inherits tier 1's calling
convention either, so what the two actually share shrank to the error
mapping, the registry/activation story and the command scaffolding —
work tier 2 must do anyway.

so: build tier 1 first if the goal is a good general facility with the
cheapest first increment. build plans/scala_mcp_tool first if the goal is
THE LINTER, because tier 1 is then a detour, not a stepping stone.
(recorded 2026-08-19 — the linter is the stated near-term goal, so tier 2
is the live path.)

depends on plans/mcp_tool_command (DONE — the outer syntax, the params
clause, the quoting/validation pipeline) and plans/param_schema_v2
(DONE — the param/ptyp/annotations layer this reuses verbatim).
composes with plans/ml_builtin_migration: that plan's async MCP.run_tool
matters MORE once this lands, see A3.

NON-GOALS. no reflection (that is tier 2). no new Scala.Fun of our own.
no change to the registry, the activation attribute, bundles, tool_scope
or tools/list_changed — a scala_mcp_fun row is an ordinary registry row
and inherits all of that unchanged. no enumeration of available scala
functions in tools/list: the user names the function they want.

what already exists, and is the reason this is small
-----------------------------------------------------

VERIFIED 2026-08-19, reproducer mcp/test/repro_scala_bridge.py (7/7
green): ML can call scala from inside an mcp tool body over a live
server. the reentrancy worry — an ML tool is invoked THROUGH a protocol
command and would be replying through another — does not materialise.

ML also already has the scala function TABLE, so declaration-time
validation costs nothing:

    (* Pure/Build/resources.ML:26 *)
    val check_scala_function:
      Proof.context -> string * Position.T -> string * (bool * bool)

it resolves against #scala_functions in the session base, which Scala
ships to ML at session start (Pure/Build/resources.scala:51,57 encode
Scala.functions into the base). the returned (single, bytes) flags say
whether the Fun takes exactly one argument and whether it is Bytes- or
string-shaped. so the command can PROVE its target exists at
registration, the same way mcp_tool proves a diagnostic command with
Outer_Syntax.check_command, and a typo fails with a position instead of
at call time.

and Pure already ships the CALLING dispatch those flags are for, so we
do not design it — we copy it:

    (* Pure/Build/resources.ML:202, the \<^scala> antiquotation *)
    val (name, (single, bytes)) = check_scala_function ctxt arg;
    val func =
      (if single then "Scala.function1" else "Scala.function") ^
      (if bytes then "_bytes" else "");

this is the whole reason the Fun hierarchy exists. Fun is the real
thing (invoke: Session -> Bytes.T list -> Bytes.T list); Fun_Strings,
Fun_String and Fun_Bytes are scala-side ADAPTERS over that one wire
shape, and (single, bytes) is metadata shipped to ML precisely so the
ML side can pick the matching entry point. we did not invent any of it
and must not reinvent it.

WHAT LANDED 2026-08-19
----------------------

IMPLEMENTED and green. The command, the combinator
(MCP_Combinators.scala_fun) and the registration-time checks all exist;
cases 8a-8e of mcp/test/repro_dynamic_call.py cover them.

    scala_mcp_fun catalog = \<open>doc_names\<close>
        (description \<open>the isabelle doc catalog\<close>)

    tools/call catalog {}  ->  26 entries, matching `isabelle doc`

D1 landed exactly as decided: Pure's (single, bytes) dispatch is copied,
not reinvented, and the ONLY thing this plan decides is the ordering --
walk the declared params in order and look each name up.

The three registration-time refusals are real and tested:
  * an unknown Scala.Fun            -> Bad scala_function "..."
  * single Fun + >= 2 declared params -> "takes exactly one argument"  (A2)
  * an (optional) param              -> not allowed, positional filling
                                        cannot represent a hole         (D3)

DEVIATION: A1/A2 were labelled [bridge] on the grounds that
Resources.check_scala_function reads a session-base table that is empty
outside a live session. That reasoning was WRONG about builds -- the
ML/Scala bridge IS available at theory BUILD time (verified while
building plans/scala_mcp_tool step 1), and the function table is
populated there too. The cases are still exercised at the bridge layer
because that is where the repro lives, but an ml-unit case would also
have worked.

the work items
--------------

1. the command. `scala_mcp_fun` as outer syntax in MCP_Tools.thy
   (keyword :: thy_decl, alongside mcp_tool/mcp_resource). target is an
   embedded name with position; description/params/annotations clauses
   are the EXISTING parsers, reused, not reimplemented. at registration:
   Resources.check_scala_function on the target, and reject a
   single=false Fun when the marshalling convention (D1) needs single.
   verified by A1, A2.

2. the body combinator. MCP_Combinators gains

       scala_fun : string -> MCP_Tool.param list -> tool_body

   two steps, neither of them clever: order the incoming NAMED args by
   the declared param list (D1), then call Pure's entry point for the
   target's (single, bytes) flags. verified by T1.

3. error mapping. Scala.function raises three ways: Scala.Null (the Fun
   returned null), ERROR msg (scala-side error), Fail msg. all three
   become an mcp isError carrying the scala message, never an ML
   exception escaping into the protocol. verified by T2.

4. tests below; rebuild per rules.

5. close-out: spec checkbox, plans/README status, CHANGELOG entry.

decisions and assumptions
-------------------------

(testing process: ml-unit cases as \<^assert> theories in
mcp/Tools/Tests, bridge cases in mcp_test/src/mcp_bridge_tests.scala
extending MCP_Session_Suite — see plans/README "testing process". the
bridge layer is the one that matters here: Scala.function needs a live
session with the protocol handler, which Fake_Backend does not have.)

D1  the calling convention. it has two halves and only ONE of them is
    ours to decide.

    THE SHAPE HALF IS ALREADY SOLVED. Pure dispatches on the target's
    (single, bytes) flags — resources.ML:202, quoted above. copy it. an
    earlier draft of this plan invented an arity rule here and was
    wrong twice over: it branched on the DECLARED param count rather
    than on what the callee accepts, and it reinvented a dispatch Pure
    already ships. Fun_Strings takes a real list (Isabelle_System's
    Make_Directory, Copy_File, Rm_Tree, Bash_Functions all do), so
    "encode everything into one slot" would have made those unreachable
    while solving nothing.

    THE ORDERING HALF IS OURS, because Pure has no opinion on it: every
    \<^scala> caller is hand-written ML that writes its arguments in
    order itself. our arguments arrive as a JSON object from a client,
    so something must turn names into positions.

    DECIDED: the params clause IS the calling convention. fill the
    argument list by walking the DECLARED param list in order and
    looking up each name in the incoming args.

      Fun single   declared params   sent
      -----------  ----------------  --------------------------------
      false        n                 [v1 .. vn] in declaration order
      true         1                 [v]
      true         0                 [""]   (the Fun's own default —
                                      what the reproducer passes to
                                      doc_names)
      true         >= 2              REGISTRATION ERROR, unrepresentable

    this is a TRUST boundary and it is deliberately placed: nothing
    checks that declaration order matches the Fun's parameter order,
    because (single, bytes) does not carry parameter names or types.
    it is acceptable here and only here — tier 1's audience owns BOTH
    sides, having written the Fun and the declaration. tier 2 does not
    get this luxury and must recover types by reflection instead, so it
    does NOT inherit this rule; it inherits only the observation that
    named-to-positional is the real problem.

    CONSEQUENCE, and it must be said where a reader will edit: for a
    scala_mcp_fun row, REORDERING THE PARAMS CLAUSE CHANGES THE CALL.
    on an ordinary mcp_tool row the order is cosmetic (names are
    matched), so this is a genuine behavioural difference between the
    two kinds of row.

D3  (optional) params are FORBIDDEN on scala_mcp_fun, rejected at
    registration. this is the one hole D1's trust argument does not
    cover: the CLIENT decides whether to omit an optional argument, at
    call time, and no amount of author diligence prevents a hole in the
    middle of a positional list. the alternatives were passing "" for a
    missing optional (silent, and "" may be meaningful to the Fun) and
    requiring optionals to be trailing (workable, more machinery).
    forbid until something needs otherwise — it is the loud option and
    the cheapest to relax later.

D2  the result is the Fun's single output string, passed through
    verbatim as the tool's text content. no parsing, no json
    interpretation: a Fun that wants structure emits it and the client
    reads it. rationale: we do not know the Fun's output shape and
    guessing would break every Fun whose output is not what we guessed.

A1  [bridge] the target is checked at DECLARATION: a scala_mcp_fun
    naming a nonexistent function fails at registration with a position,
    not at call time. the negative case is the point — it is what makes
    a typo cheap.
    NOT an ml-unit case, though it looks like one:
    Resources.check_scala_function reads #scala_functions out of the
    session base, and that table is Symtab.empty until Scala ships the
    base in (Pure/Build/resources.ML:108). an \<^assert> theory built by
    isabelle build, and MCP_Heap_Suite too, would test the EMPTY-table
    path and pass for the wrong reason. it needs a live session.

A2  [bridge] the ONE mechanical check D1 leaves us: a single=true
    target declared with TWO OR MORE params is rejected at
    REGISTRATION, naming the flag and the count. it is unrepresentable
    — Fun_String routes through Library.the_single, which raises on a
    2-list — and both the flag and the count are known at declaration,
    so it must never become a call-time surprise. the negative case is
    the test; single=false with n params, and single=true with 0 or 1,
    all register fine.
    same reason as A1 for the layer: the flags come from the session
    base table, which is empty outside a live session.

A3  [bridge] the BLOCKING cost is real and measured, not assumed:
    Scala.function parks the prover thread in
    Synchronized.guarded_access (Pure/System/scala.ML) until
    Scala.result returns. a scala_mcp_fun over the `sleep` Fun
    (Pure/System/scala.scala:75, already registered; single=true,
    declared with ONE param, so it receives a real seconds string)
    demonstrates the prover is unresponsive for the duration — a second
    tools/call on an unrelated ML tool does not complete while the
    sleep is in flight.
    this is the concrete argument for async MCP.run_tool
    (plans/ml_builtin_migration) and must be recorded as a measurement,
    not left as a worry.

T1  [bridge] the round trip end to end: a theory declares
    `scala_mcp_fun catalog = \<open>doc_names\<close>`, tools/list advertises the
    row, tools/call returns the doc catalog, and the value matches what
    `isabelle doc` prints. doc_names is chosen deliberately over `echo`:
    its result is computed scala-side from the filesystem, so ML could
    not fabricate it and a passthrough cannot be mistaken for a round
    trip. mcp/test/repro_scala_bridge.py already proves the underlying
    mechanism this way; this test pins it through the new COMMAND.
    declare it with NO params: doc_names is single=true, so D1's
    zero-param row sends `[""]` — exactly what the reproducer passes.
    that also makes T1 the regression test for the rule, since
    doc_names' one argument is an ml_platform and would be silently
    misread if we ever put a payload there.

T2  [bridge] the three failure shapes (step 3) each surface as an mcp
    isError carrying the scala message: a Fun that raises, one that
    returns null, and one given an argument it rejects. no ML
    exception escapes into the protocol and the server stays up for a
    following call.

T3  [bridge] a scala_mcp_fun row is an ORDINARY registry row:
    `declare [[mcp_tools del: catalog]]` removes it from tools/list,
    a bundle re-adds it, and tool_scope_set over a repl that declared
    one serves it — i.e. the same three activation behaviours
    mcp_tool rows already have, asserted on this new kind.

Q1  RESOLVED 2026-08-19, recorded rather than dropped because the
    reasoning is the useful part: the params clause MUST allow zero
    params. a single=true Fun whose argument has a default meaning
    (doc_names' ml_platform) is only reachable that way, and those are
    common among the Funs already registered. D1's zero-param row
    exists for this.

Q2  OPEN: whether a Fun with bytes=true (Fun_Bytes, e.g. Base64.encode)
    is worth supporting. check_scala_function reports the flag, so the
    command can reject it cleanly today; the question is whether any
    real target needs binary in or out. defer until one does — a
    speculative Bytes path would double the marshalling surface for no
    caller.

